#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100795 :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5b5d65b-ec6c-51b7-9cdd-88d75d5264d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098272} {:answer/id #uuid "120fac45-9287-5ac3-9100-a665688ad407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098273} {:answer/id #uuid "2b917d15-1467-52ba-b80f-ce6601b19575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098274} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1975e-b88f-59af-b700-be8515c0465a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098275} {:answer/id #uuid "43c462fc-7958-5b11-abe9-5c053ed82ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098276}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f720a1-e094-53ee-b045-f9df2b9d62de" :an